---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import { getPath } from '../../utils/paths';

// Summarized from the broker's own operations guides, which hold the full
// procedure and are kept in step with its MirrorMaker 2 test suite.
const brokerDocs = 'https://github.com/krabka-io/krabka-broker/blob/main/docs/operations';

const differences = [
  { area: 'Wire protocol', kafka: 'Kafka protocol', krabka: 'The same protocol, byte-compatible. Clients, the admin CLI and librdkafka work unchanged.' },
  { area: 'Runtime', kafka: 'JVM', krabka: 'One native Rust binary. No heap tuning and no garbage collector.' },
  { area: 'Metadata', kafka: 'KRaft quorum', krabka: 'A native KRaft quorum (KIP-595 RPCs). No ZooKeeper.' },
  { area: 'Metrics', kafka: 'JMX MBeans', krabka: 'Prometheus on /metrics. No JMX port; each series names the MBean it replaces.' },
  { area: 'Broker plugins', kafka: 'JVM classes loaded into the broker', krabka: 'None. Custom authorizers, metrics reporters and interceptors have nothing to load into.' },
  { area: 'Correctness evidence', kafka: 'Tests', krabka: 'Tests plus Creusot-proved decision kernels and Stateright model checks.' },
];

const carried = [
  ['Records, with keys, values, headers and timestamps', 'Yes'],
  ['Topic partition counts and topic configs', 'Yes, with sync.topic.configs'],
  ['Consumer-group positions', 'Yes, translated, with sync.group.offsets.enabled=true'],
  ['Topic ACLs', 'Yes, when the source has an authorizer and sync.topic.acls is on'],
  ['Offsets as numbers', 'No: target offsets differ; read positions from krabka after cutover'],
  ['Transactional state and producer ids', 'No: restart transactional producers against krabka'],
  ['SCRAM credentials, delegation tokens, quotas', 'No: create them on krabka first'],
  ['Schema registry subjects', 'No: use the registry’s own export, or mirror _schemas and cut the registry over'],
];

const mm2 = `clusters = prod, krabka
prod.bootstrap.servers = kafka-1.example.com:9092
krabka.bootstrap.servers = krabka-1.example.com:9092

prod->krabka.enabled = true
prod->krabka.topics = .*
prod->krabka.groups = .*

sync.topic.configs.enabled = true
sync.group.offsets.enabled = true
sync.group.offsets.interval.seconds = 10
emit.checkpoints.interval.seconds = 10`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Migrating from Apache Kafka"
  description="How krabka differs from Apache Kafka, which Kafka tools work against it, and how to move a running Kafka cluster onto krabka with MirrorMaker 2."
  currentSlug="/docs/migration"
>
  <div class="space-y-6 font-sans">
    <div class="border-b border-white/10 pb-4">
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-extrabold text-white sm:text-4xl">Migrating from Apache Kafka</h1>
      <p class="text-gray-400 mt-2 text-sm max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
        What changes, what keeps working, and how to move a running cluster.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-6 text-sm text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        krabka serves the Apache Kafka wire protocol, so applications move by changing their bootstrap address. The work
        is in what sits beside the protocol: JMX, broker plugins, and the data already in your cluster. krabka is pre-1.0;
        check the <a href={getPath('/docs/broker/kip_matrix')} class="text-krab-400 hover:text-krab-300 underline underline-offset-2">KIP matrix</a>
        for the features your applications use before you plan a cutover.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-white pt-2">How krabka differs from Apache Kafka</h2>
      <div class="frosted-card rounded-xl overflow-hidden border border-white/10 not-prose">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">
          <thead class="bg-slate-950/80 border-b border-white/10 uppercase text-gray-400">
            <tr><th class="py-3 px-4">Area</th><th class="py-3 px-4">Apache Kafka</th><th class="py-3 px-4">krabka</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5">
            {differences.map((d) => (
              <tr>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 font-semibold text-white whitespace-nowrap">{d.area}</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-400">{d.kafka}</td>
                <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">{d.krabka}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        For throughput, latency and memory against Kafka and Redpanda on the same hardware, see the
        <a href={getPath('/benchmarks')} class="text-krab-400 hover:text-krab-300 underline underline-offset-2">benchmarks</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-white pt-2">Which tools keep working</h2>
      <p>
        The line is the interface a tool reads. A tool that speaks the Kafka protocol works: the
        <code class="font-mono text-krab-300">kafka-*.sh</code> admin commands, every client library, lag monitors such as
        Burrow and kafka-lag-exporter, dashboards such as AKHQ, Kafdrop and Redpanda Console, MirrorMaker 2, Kafka Connect
        workers and schema registries.
      </p>
      <p>
        A tool that needs a broker-side JVM does not: Cruise Control (use
        <a href={getPath('/docs/runtime-architecture')} class="text-krab-400 hover:text-krab-300 underline underline-offset-2">krabka-rebalancer</a>),
        JMX exporters and <code class="font-mono text-krab-300">jmx_prometheus_javaagent</code> (scrape
        <code class="font-mono text-krab-300">/metrics</code>), and the JMX views of CMAK or Conduktor. Tools with both a
        protocol path and a JMX path keep the protocol path. The full list is in
        <a href={`${brokerDocs}/ecosystem-support.md`} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-krab-400 hover:text-krab-300 underline underline-offset-2">ecosystem-support.md</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-white pt-2">Move the data with MirrorMaker 2</h2>
      <p>
        MirrorMaker 2 treats krabka as an ordinary Kafka cluster: run the stock
        <code class="font-mono text-krab-300">connect-mirror-maker.sh</code> from a Kafka release and install nothing on
        either side. The broker's test suite runs this procedure with MirrorMaker 2 from Kafka 4.3.1 on every change.
      </p>
      <div class="frosted-card rounded-xl overflow-hidden border border-white/10 not-prose">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-xs">
          <thead class="bg-slate-950/80 border-b border-white/10 uppercase text-gray-400">
            <tr><th class="py-3 px-4">What</th><th class="py-3 px-4">Carried over?</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5">
            {carried.map(([what, how]) => (
              <tr><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-200">{what}</td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">{how}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>A minimal <code class="font-mono text-krab-300">mm2.properties</code> mirroring a cluster aliased <code class="font-mono text-krab-300">prod</code>:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={mm2} lang="properties" />
      <p>
        Set <code class="font-mono text-krab-300">sync.group.offsets.enabled</code> to <code class="font-mono text-krab-300">true</code>;
        without it every migrated consumer starts from the beginning or the end. Mirrored topics carry the source alias as a
        prefix (<code class="font-mono text-krab-300">prod.orders</code>) unless you choose
        <code class="font-mono text-krab-300">IdentityReplicationPolicy</code> before the first record arrives.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-white pt-2">Cut over</h2>
      <ol class="list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5">
        <li>Wait until MirrorMaker 2's consumer lag on the source stays near zero.</li>
        <li>Move consumers one group at a time: stop the group on Kafka, wait one <code class="font-mono text-krab-300">sync.group.offsets.interval.seconds</code>, check its position on krabka with <code class="font-mono text-krab-300">kafka-consumer-groups.sh --describe</code>, then start it against krabka and confirm its lag falls.</li>
        <li>Move producers last: stop them on Kafka, let the mirror drain, start them against krabka, then stop MirrorMaker 2.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Moving producers before their consumers splits a topic across two clusters. The step-by-step procedure, including
        ACL sync and cleanup, is
        <a href={`${brokerDocs}/migrate-from-kafka.md`} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-krab-400 hover:text-krab-300 underline underline-offset-2">migrate-from-kafka.md</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
